<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Longfellow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow was a nineteenth-century American poet, author of works such as The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> corresponded with him. The site cites this link as an example of Pedro II's personal intellectual engagement with the wider world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow.jpg" alt="Henry Wadsworth Longfellow" width="304" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julia Margaret Cameron · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Wadsworth_Longfellow,_photographed_by_Julia_Margaret_Cameron_in_1868.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Longfellow was born on 27 February 1807 in Portland, then a town of the District of Maine in Massachusetts. His father, Stephen Longfellow, was a lawyer, and his maternal grandfather, Peleg Wadsworth, had been a general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> and a member of Congress. He was the second of eight children and descended from English colonists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, among them Mayflower Pilgrims. A studious child fluent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, he published his first poem at the age of 13, a patriotic piece in the Portland Gazette on 17 November 1820.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After graduating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bowdoin-college">Bowdoin College</a> in 1825 he was offered a professorship of modern languages there. He toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from May 1826 to August 1829, learned French, Italian, Spanish, Portuguese and German mostly without formal instruction, and met Washington Irving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, who encouraged him to write. At Bowdoin he translated textbooks, and his first published book, in 1833, was a translation of the poems of the medieval Spanish poet Jorge Manrique. He later taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard College</a>. His collections Voices of the Night (1839) and Ballads and Other Poems (1841) made him instantly popular. He retired from teaching in 1854 to devote himself to writing.</p><p>His first wife, Mary Potter, died in 1835 after a miscarriage. In 1843 he married Frances Appleton, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> industrialist Nathan Appleton, after a long courtship. She died on 10 July 1861 after her dress caught fire, and Longfellow, burned while trying to save her, could not attend her funeral. He grew a beard from then on. He turned for a time to translation and, from 1864, met friends every Wednesday in the Dante Club to refine his version of Dante Alighieri's Divine Comedy, published in three volumes in 1867. He was the first American to translate it completely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Longfellow wrote lyric poems known for their musicality, many telling stories from mythology and legend, and he used many forms, including hexameter, blank verse, ballads and sonnets. His best-known works include Evangeline (1847), The Song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a> (1855) and Paul Revere's Ride. He supported abolitionism and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, hoped for reconciliation between North and South. His poem Christmas Bells later became the basis of a carol. He died on 24 March 1882.</p><p>He was the most popular American poet of his day and had success overseas. His seventieth birthday in 1877 took on the air of a national holiday. Soon after his death his reputation declined, as academics turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walt-whitman">Walt Whitman</a>, Robert Frost and others, and critics judged his verse sentimental and derivative of European models. In 2020 Nicholas A. Basbanes defended him as the victim of an orchestrated dismissal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Wadsworth Longfellow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pedro II studied languages including Hebrew, Sanskrit and Tupi, and corresponded with Louis Pasteur and <b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b> was its first American translator, and it has been translated into English more times than into any other language, and poets including Seamus Heaney, Robert Pinsky and W. S. Merwin have translated all or parts of it.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The song itself has been translated into several languages, among them an English version published by the American poet <b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b> and Robert Bridges made further translations.</blockquote><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Portland, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Cambridge, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.66,-70.26&amp;year=1855" aria-label="Portland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2862 616 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry Wadsworth Longfellow"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3312 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3312 886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.66,-70.26&amp;year=1855">What was here in 1855? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Lives of great men all remind us We can make our lives sublime, And, departing, leave behind us Footprints on the sands of time…&#8221;</p><footer>A Psalm of Life (1838) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thou, too, sail on, O Ship of State! Sail on, O UNION, strong and great! Humanity with all its fears, With all the hopes of future years, Is hanging breathless on thy fate!&#8221;</p><footer>The Building of the Ship (1849) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ships that pass in the night, and speak each other in passing, Only a signal shown and a distant voice in the darkness; So on the ocean of life we pass and speak one another…&#8221;</p><footer>Tales of a Wayside Inn, part 3 (1873), the Theologian's tale &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Toiling,—rejoicing,—sorrowing, Onward through life he goes; Each morning sees some task begin, Each evening sees it close Something attempted, something done, Has earned a night's repose.&#8221;</p><footer>The Village Blacksmith (1840) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Under a spreading chestnut-tree The village smithy stands; The smith, a mighty man is he, With large and sinewy hands; And the muscles of his brawny arms Are strong as iron bands.&#8221;</p><footer>The Village Blacksmith (1840) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
